{"document":3,"title":"user in #gpt4_4ef30696:4067bede: I'm trying to organize my digital life, can you he...","updated_at":"2022-01-16T03:16:00+00:00","source_type":"slack","content":"What a great idea! Creating a spreadsheet to track your reading list will help you stay organized and focused on your reading goals. Here's a simple template to get you started: **Book Recommendations from Margaret** | **Book Title** | **Author** | **Priority Level** | | --- | --- | --- | |  |  |  | You can add columns as needed, but these three should give you a good starting point. Here's a brief explanation for each column: 1. **Book Title**: The title of the book recommended by Margaret. 2. **Author**: The author of the book. 3. **Priority Level**: A ranking system to help you prioritize which books to read first. You can use numbers (1-5), letters (A-E), or even emojis (e.g., ⭐️, 👍, 📚) to indicate the level of priority. To make it even more organized, you could add additional columns, such as: * **Genre**: The book's genre (e.g., fiction, non-fiction, mystery, sci-fi, fantasy). * **Status**: A column to track your progress (e.g., \"To-Read\", \"Reading\", \"Finished\", \"DNF\" (did not finish)). * **Notes**: A column for any notes or comments you want to make about the book. * **Source**: A column to note where you heard about the book (in this case, \"Margaret's Recommendation\"). Here's an example of what the spreadsheet might look like with a few entries: | **Book Title** | **Author** | **Priority Level** | | --- | --- | --- | | The Nightingale | Kristin Hannah | ⭐️ | | The Seven Husbands of Evelyn Hugo | Taylor Jenkins Reid | 👍 | | The Power | Naomi Alderman | 📚 |","metadata":"{\"Channel\": \"gpt4_4ef30696:4067bede\"}"}
